.\" umem_inspect(3) -- libumem runtime introspection API.
.TH UMEM_INSPECT 3 "2025-01" "libumem" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
umem_findleaks, umem_log_dump, umem_status_dump, umem_whatis,
umem_bufctl_audit_dump, umem_walk_allocated, umem_walk_freed,
umem_walk_log, umem_walk_dump, umem_inspect_snapshot,
umem_inspect_enable_events, umem_inspect_set_event_cb,
umem_event_alloc, umem_event_free, umem_event_error \-
runtime introspection for libumem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <umem_inspect.h>
.PP
.BI "size_t umem_findleaks(FILE *" out ", umem_inspect_format_t " fmt ,
.BI "    unsigned " max_classes );
.BI "size_t umem_log_dump(FILE *" out ", umem_inspect_format_t " fmt ,
.BI "    unsigned " max_records );
.BI "void   umem_status_dump(FILE *" out ", umem_inspect_format_t " fmt );
.BI "int    umem_whatis(const void *" addr ", umem_buffer_info_t *" out );
.BI "int    umem_bufctl_audit_dump(FILE *" out ", const void *" addr );
.PP
.BI "size_t umem_walk_allocated(umem_buffer_cb_t " cb ", void *" arg );
.BI "size_t umem_walk_freed(umem_buffer_cb_t " cb ", void *" arg );
.BI "size_t umem_walk_log(umem_buffer_cb_t " cb ", void *" arg );
.BI "size_t umem_walk_dump(FILE *" out ", const char *" kind ,
.BI "    umem_inspect_format_t " fmt ", unsigned " max_entries );
.PP
.BI "int    umem_inspect_snapshot(const char *" path );
.PP
.BI "void   umem_inspect_enable_events(int " on );
.BI "void   umem_inspect_set_event_cb(umem_event_cb_t " cb ", void *" arg ,
.BI "    unsigned " event_mask );
.BI "void   umem_event_alloc(void *" buf ", size_t " size ", void *" cache );
.BI "void   umem_event_free (void *" buf ", size_t " size ", void *" cache );
.BI "void   umem_event_error(int " code ", void *" buf ", void *" cache );
.fi
.PP
Link with
.BR \-lumem .
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B umem_inspect
family of functions exposes the introspection state that libumem
already maintains under
.B UMEM_DEBUG
and
.B UMEM_LOGGING
to application code, debuggers, and external tools.  These are the
non-Solaris equivalents of the
.BR mdb (1)
.BR ::findleaks ", " ::umem_log ", " ::umastat ", " ::whatis ", and"
.B ::bufctl_audit
dcmds.
.PP
All routines that take a
.B FILE *
argument may be passed
.BR NULL ;
output is then written to
.BR stderr .
.PP
.B LOCKING AND SNAPSHOT CONTRACT.
Every cache-list walk holds
.B umem_cache_lock
for its whole duration, so a cache cannot be destroyed underneath it.
Inspection therefore blocks
.BR umem_cache_create (3)
and
.BR umem_cache_destroy (3)
while it collects; it does not block allocation.
.PP
Collection is two-phase: buffers are copied under the relevant locks into a
buffer sized beforehand, and then formatting, sorting, classification, file
I/O and any caller-supplied walk callback run with every allocator lock
released.  A walk callback may therefore allocate, perform I/O, and call back
into libumem.
.PP
A report is consistent per cache but is
.I not
one instant across caches: cache A is read before cache B, and allocation
continues in between.  If the allocation set grows faster than it can be
snapshotted the report says so explicitly \(em
.I truncated
in JSON \(em rather than silently under-reporting.
.PP
Statistics counters are read under the lock that writes them, so each is
whole, but a set of them is not a single instant.  The rseq per-CPU magazine
state has no mutex by construction and is read best-effort with clamped
counts.  The transaction log's
.B lh_lock
protects only chunk rotation \(em records are written under a per-CPU lock, so
a record can be observed half-written; implausible records are discarded
rather than reported.
.PP
Because these routines take allocator mutexes, calling them from a debugger
against a target that was stopped while one of those mutexes was held will
hang the call.  Retry, or take a snapshot from inside the process.
.SS umem_findleaks
Walks every user-visible cache, enumerates buffers that are currently held,
groups them by allocation stack-trace fingerprint, and
emits the top
.I max_classes
classes ranked by total bytes.  Returns the number of buffers
visited.
.PP
Buffers currently sitting in magazines are detected and subtracted (and
reported as
.I cached_skipped
in the JSON output), and allocator-internal caches
.RB ( UMC_NOHASH ", " UMC_QCACHE )
are filtered out.
.PP
The result is
.I outstanding allocations,
which is not the same as leaks.  The cached-set computation covers the
central depot, the per-CPU depot arrays, each per-CPU loaded/previous
magazine, and each rseq per-CPU loaded/previous magazine.  It does
.I not
cover buffers retained in another thread's per-thread cache (PTC): those bins
are thread-local with no process-wide registry, so no other thread can
enumerate them.  When PTC is enabled the JSON report sets
.I ptc_unaccounted
and the text report states it; run the target with
.B UMEM_OPTIONS=tcache=0
for an exact count.  Oversize allocations \(em above the largest size class,
served directly from a vmem arena rather than from a cache \(em are not
accounted here at all.  A buffer the application legitimately still owns is
also outstanding.  Use the count as an upper bound, and compare reports over
time rather than reading a single one as a verdict.
.PP
Stack traces are present only when the target was started with
.BR UMEM_DEBUG=audit ;
without audit the buffers are still counted but the stacks are empty, so
classes cannot be distinguished from one another.
.SS umem_log_dump
Walks the per-CPU transaction-log ring buffer, sorts its entries by
.BR bc_timestamp ,
and emits each one (alloc or free event with thread, time, address,
size, cache, and stack).  Requires
.BR UMEM_LOGGING=transaction= \fIsize\fR
in the target environment;
.I size
accepts
.BR k ", " m ", or " g
suffixes.  Returns the number of records emitted.
.SS umem_status_dump
Per-cache summary equivalent to mdb's
.BR ::umastat :
buffer size, buffers
.B HELD
above the slab layer, total, memory in use, allocations,
allocation failures, and depot contention.
.PP
.B HELD
(
.I inuse
in JSON) is
.IR cache_slab_alloc " \- " cache_slab_free .
A buffer the application has already freed but which still sits in a magazine,
an rseq magazine or a PTC bin is counted here, so this is not a measure of
application memory in use.  The
.I alloc_ops
field is recomputed by summing the per-CPU counters; the
.B cache_alloc_ops
field it used to report is only refreshed inside an optional magazine-tuning
branch and otherwise reads zero or stale.
.SS umem_whatis
Resolves
.I addr
to its owning cache, slab, and current state:
.B UMEM_BUF_ALLOCATED
(held),
.B UMEM_BUF_FREE
(on a slab freelist), or
.B UMEM_BUF_CACHED
(freed by the application but still resident in a magazine, depot magazine or
rseq magazine).  Fills
.I *out
on success.  Prefers user-visible caches over the qcaches that back
them: an address inside an
.B umem_va_4096
buffer that is itself a slab for
.B umem_alloc_512
will resolve to
.BR umem_alloc_512 .
Returns 0 on success, \-1 if
.I addr
is not in any libumem region (with
.B errno
set to
.BR ENOENT ).
.PP
A buffer retained in another thread's PTC bin reports
.BR UMEM_BUF_ALLOCATED ,
since thread-local bins are not enumerable from outside the owning thread.
That is the one documented false positive.
.SS umem_bufctl_audit_dump
Pretty-prints the bufctl audit record (as the mdb dcmd
.B ::bufctl_audit
does) for the buffer at
.IR addr .
Includes alloc thread, timestamp, and the captured stack trace when
.B UMEM_DEBUG=audit
is in effect.
.SS umem_walk_allocated, umem_walk_freed, umem_walk_log
Streaming walkers.  The callback
.I cb
is invoked once per entry with a
.B umem_buffer_info_t
that the callback may use, copy, or ignore.  Returning a non-zero
value from
.I cb
stops the walk early.
.PP
The callback runs against a snapshot, with
.B no allocator lock held,
so it may allocate, perform I/O, and call back into libumem.  It previously
ran under each cache's lock, which deadlocks under malloc interposition.
.SS umem_walk_dump
Convenience wrapper that streams every entry in
.I kind
.RB ( "\(dqallocated\(dq" ", " "\(dqfreed\(dq" ", or " "\(dqlog\(dq" )
to
.I out
in the requested format.
.SS umem_inspect_snapshot
Writes a self-contained snapshot of allocator state to
.IR path .
If the path ends in
.IR .ums ", " .umsnap ", or " .bin
the v2 binary format is written (parseable offline by the
.B umem_dump_reader
helper); otherwise a human-readable text dump is produced.  Useful
from
.BR atexit (3),
signal handlers, or admin endpoints.  Returns 0 on success, \-1 on
I/O error.
.SS umem_inspect_enable_events, umem_inspect_set_event_cb
Toggle the event-hook subsystem.  When enabled, every alloc, free,
and detected-corruption event calls a stable extern function
.RB ( umem_event_alloc ", " umem_event_free ", " umem_event_error ).
Set a debugger breakpoint on these to break on memory events with
arbitrary conditions.
.PP
A C callback may also be registered with
.B umem_inspect_set_event_cb;
.I event_mask
is a bitmask of
.BR "UMEM_EV_ALLOC" ", " UMEM_EV_FREE ", " UMEM_EV_DOUBLE_FREE ", "
.BR UMEM_EV_CORRUPT ", " UMEM_EV_ALLOC_FAIL .
.PP
When events are off (the default) the hook functions are reached but
return immediately with ~1 cycle of overhead.
.SS Event hook breakpoints
The functions
.BR umem_event_alloc ", " umem_event_free ", and " umem_event_error
are intentionally simple stable symbols.  They are the natural target
of a debugger breakpoint:
.PP
.RS
.nf
(gdb) break umem_event_alloc if size >= 1048576
(gdb) break umem_event_free  if buf == 0x7fa9b0c00000
(gdb) break umem_event_error
.fi
.RE
.PP
Or via the
.BR umem (1)
command-line wrapper:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ umem --pid 12345 findleaks -f json | jq .
.fi
.RE
.SH RETURN VALUES
Routines documented as returning
.B size_t
return the number of items processed.  Routines documented as
returning
.B int
return 0 on success and \-1 on failure (with
.B errno
set).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B UMEM_DEBUG=audit
Required for stack traces (every alloc/free records up to
.I umem_stack_depth
PCs into the bufctl audit record).
.TP
.BI UMEM_LOGGING=transaction= SIZE
Required for
.B umem_log_dump
to return records.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR umem (1),
.BR umem_alloc (3),
.BR umem_cache_create (3),
.BR umem_debug (3),
.BR umem_debugging (7),
.BR umem_dump_reader (1),
.BR mdb (1)
.PP
.I tools/DEBUGGING.md
in the libumem source distribution.
.SH HISTORY
The introspection API was added to the libumem revival in 2025 as the
non-Solaris equivalent of the
.BR mdb (1)
.B ::findleaks
and
.B ::umem_log
dcmds.  Prior to it, debugging libumem-using processes on Linux meant
either core-dumping and groveling through 1\ MB of audit ring buffer
by hand, or giving up and using
.BR valgrind (1).
